speechtext:
Yes. The other strong point which Sir John French made was that the training, or the education, both at the schools and in the camps, was not up to date, and he made some suggestions with regard to these matters, which will be carried into effect and which,
I think, are very desirable. As a result of his criticisms, I am glad to say, that 269}
Major General Mackenzie is giving special attention to this matter. He proposes to take pains to see that officers from the permanent force shall be sent to the different parts of the country where they may be required in order to give the officers of the active militia an opportunity of learning their duties, so that they will be able to take their positions in the different units, regiments, &c., throughout the country, with advantage to themselves and to the militia generally.
